\section{A new reader}

\ltnote{%
  Relevant learning objectives: \cref{AiLOReader}.

  The goals stay invariant through the whole session, as they did for the
  systems in the lecture on what literate programming is, so that what varies
  is
  the reader and the approach.
  \Cref{ex:ai-matter} comes before we say anything about agents as readers: the
  answers show whether students already separate the human from the agent,
  which
  is the critical aspect of \cref{AiLOReader}.
  We don't point it out in the question.
}%
You have already met the two goals of literate programming.
Both are about people.

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{block}{The two goals}
    \LPgoals
  \end{block}
  \begin{exercise}\label{ex:ai-matter}
\begin{pycode}
ai_poll(
    open_text,
    "When agents write most of the code, does explaining a program to human "
    "beings matter more, less or about the same? Why?",
    context=AI_LP_GOALS,
)
\end{pycode}
  \end{exercise}
\end{frame}

Look at the reasons in your answers, because they tend to assume different
readers.
Someone who answers \enquote{more} often has a person in mind, who must now
understand code they didn't write.
Someone who answers \enquote{less} often has in mind that nobody will read the
code any more.
A third reader has appeared, too: the agent itself reads the prose when it next
works on the program.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{block}{Who reads the prose?}
    \begin{description}
      \item[The maintainer] A person who has to understand and change the
        program, as Knuth had in mind.
      \item[The agent] Prose is part of the context it works from.
      \item[The checker] A person who has to decide whether what an agent wrote
        is right, often without having written any of it.
    \end{description}
  \end{block}
  \begin{remark}
    What helps one of them need not help another.
    So when someone says that documentation helps, ask: helps whom, to do what?
  \end{remark}
\end{frame}

\section{What does the research say?}

\ltnote{%
  Relevant learning objectives: \cref{AiLOEvidence}.

  \Cref{ex:ai-research-amount} asks for a guess before any paper is shown.
  Popular accounts of agents and literate programming are confident, so we
  expect
  many to overestimate how much has been tested.
  The gap between the guess and the answer is the point: it makes the kind of
  evidence visible as something to ask about.
}%
Since 2023 a number of researchers and practitioners have connected literate
programming with language models.
Before we look, guess how much of it has been tested.

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{exercise}\label{ex:ai-research-amount}
\begin{pycode}
ai_poll(
    mc,
    "Since 2023, how much research has tested literate programming together "
    "with language models or coding agents?",
    [
        "Many controlled studies",
        "A few controlled studies",
        "Mostly proposals and tools, with little evaluation",
        "Nothing at all",
    ],
)
\end{pycode}
  \end{exercise}
\end{frame}

To answer it, we have to sort claims by the kind of evidence behind them.
We searched the literature from 2023 onwards for that purpose, in five
databases, by following the papers that cite Knuth's, and through the reference
lists of the papers we found; \cref{app:ai-method} says how, and
\cref{app:ai-rq1,app:ai-rq23,app:ai-rq4} give each search in full.
\Cref{def:ai-evidence} gives the three kinds we sort by.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{definition}[Three kinds of evidence]\label{def:ai-evidence}
    \begin{description}
      \item[Proposal] An idea or a tool, perhaps with a demonstration, but no
        measurement of whether it works.
      \item[Benchmark] A measurement of what a model does on a set of tasks.
        It says nothing directly about people.
      \item[Study with people] A measurement of what people do or understand,
        ideally against a control group.
    \end{description}
  \end{definition}
\end{frame}

Most of what we found are proposals.
Google proposes that a model write a short outline of each function in prose,
interleaved with the code, as a new way to read and navigate
it\autocite{Shi2025NLO}.
\Textcite{Zhang2024ILP} propose literate documents as the prompts for a model
working on a large project.
\Textcite{Yuan2026VLP} propose literate documentation that a person can check
instead of the code.
And \textcite{Burke2026notlob} build a literate programming environment for
humans and agents together, noting that \textcquote[sec.~1]{Burke2026notlob}{%
experimental evaluation is left to future work}.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{block}{What has been proposed}
    \begin{description}
      \item[Outlines] A model writes the prose outline of each
        function\autocite{Shi2025NLO}.
      \item[Literate prompts] Literate documents as the context a model works
        from\autocite{Zhang2024ILP}.
      \item[Checkable prose] Documentation a person checks instead of the
        code\autocite{Yuan2026VLP}.
      \item[An environment] Literate programs written by humans and agents
        together, not yet evaluated\autocite{Burke2026notlob}.
    \end{description}
  \end{block}
\end{frame}

\ltnote{%
  Each item below is placed on \cref{def:ai-evidence}, so that the same three
  categories are applied to four different results, and the result of
  \cref{ex:ai-research-amount} gets its answer one study at a time.
}%
Only four of the works measure anything, and each measures something narrower
than its title suggests.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{block}{What has been measured}
    \begin{description}
      \item[Outlines] In the best of ten setups, six raters, five of them
        the authors, rated outlines of 30 functions: 60\,\% excellent,
        90\,\% acceptable\autocite{Shi2025NLO}.
      \item[Checkable prose] Large gains with a model playing the user and
        seeing the answer; the study with people, eight participants, also
        found gains\autocite{Yuan2026VLP}.
      \item[Literate comments] Removing them did not change accuracy, in one
        benchmark with one model\autocite{RossiPrestwich2026}.
      \item[\enquote{Literate} models] Whether a model's internal
        representations tell languages and tasks apart: another meaning of
        the word\autocite{SunStaron2026}.
    \end{description}
  \end{block}
\end{frame}

So a model can write prose about code that its authors rate as good.
In the study of checkable prose, the gains were measured with a simulated user
who sees the reference solution, since \textcquote[sec.~V-A]{Yuan2026VLP}{The
oracle context includes the benchmark prompt and golden reference
implementation}.
When \textcite{RossiPrestwich2026} took the literate-style comments away from
their system, the \textcquote[sec.~5]{RossiPrestwich2026}{ablation of
literate-style comments \textelp{} did not produce differences in accuracy with
respect to the baseline}.
The only journal article in the set asks whether a model is
\enquote{literate}, in the sense that it recognises both the language and the
task of a program, which is not about writing literate programs at
all\autocite{SunStaron2026}.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{remark}[Two things nobody has done yet]
    \begin{itemize}
      \item Where an evaluated system has prose, a model writes it, mostly
        from the code.
        That is the reverse of Knuth's order.
      \item We found no controlled test of literate programming as Knuth meant
        it, with the explanation written first, together with a language
        model.
    \end{itemize}
  \end{remark}
\end{frame}

The answer to \cref{ex:ai-research-amount} is the third option: mostly
proposals
and tools, with little evaluation.
That does not mean literate programming doesn't help with agents.
It means we found no measurement of it yet, which is a different thing.

\subsection{Prose for the agent}\label{sec:ai-agent-reader}

\ltnote{%
  Relevant learning objectives: \cref{AiLOReader,AiLOEvidence}.

  The options separate the critical aspect, whether the prose carries correct
  information the model lacks, from the popular belief that more comments are
  better.
  The prompt names neither.
}%
Go back to the three readers.
The agent reads the prose too, so does it help the agent?

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{exercise}\label{ex:ai-comments-agent}
\begin{pycode}
ai_poll(
    mc,
    "When does a comment in the code help a language model write better code?",
    [
        "Always: more explanation gives better code",
        "When it tells the model something correct that it couldn't otherwise "
        "know",
        "When the model has written the comment itself",
        "Never: the model only looks at the code",
    ],
)
\end{pycode}
  \end{exercise}
\end{frame}

Here the benchmarks are fairly clear, and point to the second option.
\Textcite{pan2026talking} controlled what the comments said.
\textcquote[abstract]{pan2026talking}{Comments from source solutions that pass
the tests raise recipient pass@1 by 17.2\,\% on average.
In contrast, comments describing failed solutions provide no reliable gain,
while comments written for a different problem reduce pass@1 by 20.8\,\%.}
Asking the model to write its own comments recovered at most about a quarter of
that gain.
Prose about something else is worse than none.
Giving models the docstring of another function,
\textcite{macke2024documentation}
found that
\textcquote[abstract]{macke2024documentation}{providing an LLM with
\enquote{incorrect} documentation can greatly hinder code understanding, while
incomplete or missing documentation does not seem to significantly affect an
LLM's ability to understand code}.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{block}{What prose does for the agent}
    \begin{description}
      \item[Correct and new] Helps: comments from passing solutions raised
      small
        models' pass rate by about 17 percentage
        points\autocite{pan2026talking}, and comments gave up to three times as
        many exact bug
        fixes, from about 3 to 10\,\%, in fine-tuned models and with comments
        that partly describe the fix\autocite{vitale2026comments}.
      \item[Off-topic] Hurts, more than no prose at
        all\autocite{pan2026talking,macke2024documentation}; a wrong solution
        described in comments gave no gain\autocite{pan2026talking}.
      \item[Restating the code] Little or nothing: with the code in front of
        it, extra documentation didn't help an agent fix
        issues\autocite{arman2026compact}.
    \end{description}
  \end{block}
\end{frame}

What matters to the model is what the prose says, not that there is prose.
Prose that only repeats the code gives it nothing it doesn't already have.
These are benchmarks with today's models, though, and none of them used a
literate program in Knuth's sense.

\subsection{Prose for the checker}\label{sec:ai-checker}

\ltnote{%
  Relevant learning objectives: \cref{AiLOReader,AiLOEvidence}.

  This is the reader most students will be, so it gets the most careful
  treatment.
  We show the positive study first, and then the one that varied the quality of
  the explanation, since the contrast between them is about what was varied,
  not
  about who is right.
}%
The third reader decides whether what the agent wrote is right.
Does an explanation help a person do that?
Here there are studies with people, and they point in different directions.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{block}{What prose does for the checker}
    \begin{description}
      \item[Correct explanations, next to the code] Participants answered 90
        instead of 65\,\% of questions right, compared with using a
        chatbot\autocite{yan2024ivie}.
      \item[Comments of varying quality] No better judgement, even with exact
        comments; with under-specified ones, people judged less accurately
        than with exact ones, yet more confidently than with no comment at
        all\autocite{kaufman2026assertions}.
      \item[Comments and adoption] People adopt AI-written code more often when
        it has comments\autocite{li2026comments}.
    \end{description}
  \end{block}
\end{frame}

The positive result compared explanations with a chatbot, and did not vary
whether they were correct.
\Textcite{kaufman2026assertions} instead varied how good the AI-written
comments
were, in a pre-registered experiment with 86 developers.
\textcquote[sec.~RQ2]{kaufman2026assertions}{Contrary to our pre-registered
hypothesis \textelp{}, the presence of a comment (even if that comment was of
high-quality) did not significantly improve participant accuracy compared to
providing no comment at all}.
With under-specified comments, people judged the AI's output less accurately
than with exact ones, and yet more confidently than with no comment at all.
That is a preprint, and a single study, but it is the one that tested the
situation that matters most: an explanation that might be wrong.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{remark}
    An explanation can make you more confident without making you more
    correct.
    We will see an example of how this could happen in our own demonstration.
  \end{remark}
\end{frame}

There is a further reason for care.
In a randomized study by Anthropic researchers, people learning a new library
\textcquote[sec.~1.1]{shen2026skill}{using AI assistance to complete tasks that
involve this new library resulted in a reduction in the evaluation score by
17\,\% or two grade points}, measured on a test of understanding.
Reading prose about the code is not the same as understanding the code, and we
found no test of whether writing literate programs with an agent changes that.


\subsection{Prose that instructs the agent}\label{sec:ai-instructions}

\ltnote{%
  Relevant learning objectives: \cref{AiLOReader,AiLOEvidence}.

  The instruction files are the kind of prose I rely on most, so we test them
  on
  ourselves.
  \Cref{ex:ai-stale} has a plausible distractor in each direction: that
  noticing
  the conflict makes the agent follow the code, and that it compromises.
  The fourth option was impossible in the experiment, which ran without a user
  to
  ask, so we say so when revealing the answer.
}%
So far the prose was about the code.
An instruction file, such as an [[AGENTS.md]] or a [[CLAUDE.md]], tells the
agent what to do
instead.
What happens when it is out of date?

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{exercise}\label{ex:ai-stale}
\begin{pycode}
ai_poll(
    mc,
    "A project's CLAUDE.md says to report warnings with `print()`, but every "
    "module in the code uses `logging`. An agent adds a feature, and in its "
    "final reply it points out the conflict. In what share of its choices did "
    "it follow the instruction file?",
    [
        "About 5 percent: it noticed, so it followed the code",
        "About 50 percent: it split the difference",
        "About 86 percent: it noticed, and followed the file anyway",
        "It stopped and asked the user",
    ],
)
\end{pycode}
  \end{exercise}
\end{frame}

The answer is the third.
In a controlled experiment, \textcquote[abstract]{xu2026stale}{Without the
sentence, 90.1\,\% of the agents' choices followed the stale file.
Claude Code pointed out the conflict in 79 of 80 runs and still followed the
file.}
For Claude Code alone, the share in the option: it
\textcquote[sec.~5.2]{xu2026stale}{named the conflict in its final reply in 79
of 80
runs, and in those runs 86.2\,\% of its choices still matched the file}.
The sentence mentioned there was a single instruction to prefer the code when
the two disagree, which turned the result around.
Asking was not possible, since the runs had no user.
Another study found the same:
\textcquote[abstract]{mohammadi2026workingset}{where
standard and code disagree, agents follow the standard even when it prescribes
the worse code, so a stale convention file costs more than no file}.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{block}{What instruction files do}
    \begin{description}
      \item[Context files] No significant change in whether four agents solved
        the task, at about 20\,\% higher cost\autocite{gloaguen2026agentsmd}.
      \item[Out of date] Followed over the code, even when the agent notices
        the conflict\autocite{xu2026stale,mohammadi2026workingset}.
      \item[Skills] Skills written by people helped on specialised tasks, and
        those the agent wrote itself made things
        worse\autocite{li2026skillsbench};
        on ordinary repositories, 92 of 98 public skills had no significant
        effect\autocite{han2026sweskills}.
    \end{description}
  \end{block}
\end{frame}

\Textcite{gloaguen2026agentsmd} found that
\textcquote[abstract]{gloaguen2026agentsmd}{providing
context files does not generally improve task success rates, while increasing
inference cost by over 20\,\% on average}.
Be careful with that paper, though: its first version said that context files
reduce success, and only the later version says that they don't improve it.
Our demonstration shows a similar rise in cost, for a skill rather than a
context file.

One more result connects this directly to literate programming.
Instruction files keep growing, and \textcite{chakrabarti2026growing} argue
that
it is because the reason for each rule is lost, so nobody dares remove one.
They ask: \textcquote[abstract]{chakrabarti2026growing}{If English is the new
code, why don't we have comments yet?}

\begin{frame}
  \begin{remark}[A pattern across the studies]
    Prose helps an agent when it carries information the agent can't get from
    the code or already knows: a correct requirement, a procedure, a reason.
    Prose that restates the code costs tokens and does little.
    Instructions that are wrong are followed.
  \end{remark}
\end{frame}

That pattern is our reading of the studies together, not something one of them
tested.
It is, though, close to what Knuth asked of the prose: explain what the code
can't.


