The rest lives in a \emph{skill}: a text file of instructions, which the agent
loads when it is about to work on a [[.nw]] file.
Mine contains rules like the one below.
In the file, each is followed by a bad and a good version of the same
paragraph.

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{example}[From my \texttt{literate-programming} skill]
    The rule on explaining \emph{why} comes with a self-test:
    \inputminted[fontsize=\scriptsize,breaklines]%
      {markdown}{excerpts/skill-why.md}
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

\ltnote{%
  Drafted by Claude from the skill's version history: the dates and the commit
  count are facts from the repository, but the reading of them as a record of
  failures is for Daniel to confirm.
}%
The skill wasn't written in one go.
Its directory has had 72 commits since November 2025, many of them adding a
rule.
So its history doubles as a record of what agents get wrong when left to
themselves.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{block}{A year of rules}
    \begin{description}
      \item[2025-12] Introduce structure gradually, don't dump it.
      \item[2026-02] Explain why, not what, with bad and good examples.
      \item[2026-02] A chunk reused elsewhere may lack what it depends on.
      \item[2026-03] Break classes up method by method.
      \item[2026-05] Give the whole first, and keep overviews in sync.
      \item[2026-06] A stray \texttt{@} between chunks leaves a gap in the PDF.
    \end{description}
  \end{block}
\end{frame}

\ltnote{%
