Why do it, then, if it takes this much instruction?
Here is my reasoning, which is not evidence.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{block}{Why I do it}
    \begin{itemize}
      \item The explanation and the code change in the same diff, so I review
        the agent's reasons along with its code.
      \item The next agent reads the same explanation as the next person, so
        there is no second document to drift away.
      \item A wrong reason is often easier to spot than a wrong line.
    \end{itemize}
  \end{block}
  \begin{remark}
    That is reasoning from one person who chose the method.
    So let's test a small part of it.
  \end{remark}
\end{frame}

\section{The same task, three instructions}

\ltnote{%
  Relevant learning objectives: \cref{AiLOInstruct,AiLOReader}.

  \textbf{Variation pattern}: contrast.
  \textbf{What varies}: the instruction, in three values.
  \textbf{What stays invariant}: the task, the model, the tools and the
  isolation
  from any other instructions.
  The critical aspect is that what we tell the agent shapes the explanation and
  the design, while behaviour may stay the same.
  Three repetitions each keep one lucky run from carrying the conclusion, but
  this
  is an illustration, not a study: three runs of one task with one model.
}%
Here is a task that is small enough to run in front of you, and has a few
traps:
bookings can overlap, touch and come in any order.

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{example}[The task]\label{ex:ai-task}
    Every run of the agent gets this text:
<<the task>>=
Write a Python program freeslots.py that reads booked time slots from
standard input, one per line like 09:00-10:30, and prints the free slots of
the working day 08:00-17:00, one per line in the same format. Bookings can
overlap, touch each other and come in any order. Include tests in
test_freeslots.py and run them with pytest.
@
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

We vary only what we add to it.
The second instruction asks for a literate program.
The third asks for the same, but also gives the agent my skill.

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{example}[What varies]\label{ex:ai-instruction}
    The second and third runs also get this text:
<<the instruction to write literately>>=
 Write it as a literate program in noweb, in the file freeslots.nw. Tangle
it to get freeslots.py and test_freeslots.py.
@
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

We have to keep everything else out of the comparison.
A coding agent normally reads your settings, your instruction files and the
name
of the directory it is in, and any of them could tell it about literate
programming.
So the script below turns off everything it can.

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{example}[Running one of the three]\label{ex:ai-run-demo}
    The script takes the instruction as its argument:
<<[[run_demo.sh]]>>=
#!/bin/bash
# Usage: run_demo.sh plain|noweb|skill
task="$(paste -sd' ' <<'_TASK'
<<the task>>
_TASK
)"
literately="$(paste -sd' ' <<'_LP'
<<the instruction to write literately>>
_LP
)"
<<the flags that isolate the agent>>
case "$1" in
  plain) "${isolated[@]}" -- "$task" ;;
  noweb) "${isolated[@]}" -- "$task$literately" ;;
  skill) "${isolated[@]}" "${skill[@]}" -- "$task$literately" ;;
esac
@
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

The flags ignore user settings, which drops the user's own plugins and hooks,
and turn off skills and external tool servers.
The model is fixed, since a different one would be a second thing varying.
For the third run, we hand it the skill file directly.
The plain runs ran in a directory whose name says nothing about literate
programming.
The agent works without asking, and reports what it did, its cost and its time
as JSON at the end.
The recorded skill runs got the skill as committed on 28 October 2026; a live
run gets whatever the file says on the day.
The [[--]] before the prompt matters: [[--allowedTools]] takes every argument
after it, and would otherwise take the prompt for a tool name.
The recorded runs were made with these flags, each in its own empty directory
and with a time limit, three of each instruction at once.

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{example}[The isolation]
    These settings are the same in all three runs:
<<the flags that isolate the agent>>=
isolated=(claude -p --output-format json --model claude-sonnet-5-5
  --setting-sources project --disable-slash-commands --strict-mcp-config
  --permission-mode acceptEdits --allowedTools
  Read Write Edit "Bash(python3:*)" "Bash(pytest:*)" "Bash(notangle:*)"
  "Bash(noweave:*)" "Bash(noroots:*)" "Bash(cpif:*)" "Bash(black:*)"
  "Bash(chmod:*)" "Bash(ls:*)" "Bash(cat:*)")
skill=(--append-system-prompt-file
  "$HOME/.claude/skills/literate-programming/SKILL.md")
@
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

\ltnote{%
  Each option is a real outcome or a plausible misconception, and several can
  be
  right, so that ticking them separates behaviour, prose and design without our
  naming those as the aspects to look at.
  Most students will probably expect the amount of prose to grow.
  Few are likely to expect the design to change, which is the point of
  \cref{ex:ai-design}.
}%
Before we run it, what do you expect?

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{exercise}\label{ex:ai-predict-demo}
\begin{pycode}
ai_poll(
    mc,
    "We give an agent this task three times: as it stands, asking for a noweb "
    "literate program, and asking for that with my skill. What will differ "
    "between the results?",
    [
        "The programs will behave differently",
        "The amount of prose will differ",
        "What the prose explains will differ",
        "The design of the program will differ",
        "Nothing that matters",
    ],
    multi=True,
    context=(
        "The task: Write a Python program freeslots.py that reads booked time "
        "slots from standard input, one per line like 09:00-10:30, and prints "
        "the free slots of the working day 08:00-17:00, one per line in the "
        "same format. Bookings can overlap, touch each other and come in any "
        "order. Include tests in test_freeslots.py and run them with pytest."
    ),
)
\end{pycode}
  \end{exercise}
\end{frame}

\mode<presentation>{%
\begin{frame}
  \begin{block}{Live}
    We run \texttt{run\_demo.sh skill} now, and read what comes out.
  \end{block}
\end{frame}
}

In the session we run the third instruction live, in an empty directory, with
[[run_demo.sh skill > run.json]].
It takes about a minute, and then we read the [[.nw]] file it wrote.
Yours will not come out exactly like the recorded run below, since the model
doesn't give the same answer twice.
So I ran each instruction three times beforehand, and this program compares all
nine runs.
It feeds every program one input that none of the agents saw, checks the
result,
and reports what each one cost.

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{example}[All nine runs]\label{ex:ai-results}
    One input none of the agents saw, then what each instruction gave, as mean
    values per run:
    \runpython[showcommand,chdir,fontsize=\scriptsize]%
      {examples/compare_runs.py}
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

So behaviour didn't differ on this input: all nine were correct, and every
agent's
own tests pass too.
The amount of prose did differ, and so did the cost: about five times that of
the plain instruction, and almost three times that of the noweb instruction
without the skill.
That is a rise like the one for instruction files in
\cref{sec:ai-instructions},
though far larger, and our nine runs can't tell how much of it comes from
reading the skill and how much from the extra work it asks for.
The reported cost is Claude Code's estimate at API prices, and small in
absolute
terms, but the ratio is what you would notice on a larger project.
\mode<article>{\subsection{How the runs are compared}}

\ltnote{%
  This subsection exists only in the notes.
  It is too long for a slide, and in the session the output is what matters,
  but
  a deck about literate programming shouldn't rest on a program it doesn't
  explain.
  We present the main program first and its helpers after, which is not the
  order
  Python needs.
}%
The comparison asks two things of each run: does the program work, and what did
the agent write?
The main program reports them per instruction, as means, since the point is to
compare instructions and not to rank single runs.

<<[[compare_runs.py]]>>=
"""Compare what each instruction gave, over all its recorded runs."""

import json
import pathlib
import re
import statistics
import subprocess
import sys

<<constants>>

<<functions>>


def main():
    print("           correct  prose  code  merge  cost($)  seconds")
    for name in INSTRUCTIONS:
        runs = sorted((DEMO / name).iterdir())
        meta = [json.loads((run / "meta.json").read_text()) for run in runs]
        lines = [count_lines(run) for run in runs]
        print(
            f"{name:>9}  "
            f"{sum(map(is_correct, runs))}/{len(runs)}      "
            f"{statistics.mean(prose for prose, _ in lines):5.0f}  "
            f"{statistics.mean(code for _, code in lines):4.0f}  "
            f"{sum(map(has_merge_step, runs))}/{len(runs)}    "
            f"{statistics.mean(m['cost_usd'] for m in meta):7.2f}  "
            f"{statistics.mean(m['seconds'] for m in meta):7.0f}"
        )


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
<<constants>>=
DEMO = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / "demo"
INSTRUCTIONS = ["plain", "noweb", "skill"]
@

An agent's own tests can't tell us whether its program works, since the same
agent decided what to test, and with what expected results.
So every program gets one input written independently of them.
It holds each trap the task names: bookings out of order, an overlap, two that
touch.
It also has one booking inside another, which is what a program that merely
tracks the end of the last booking gets wrong.
It also has what the task doesn't name, namely bookings reaching outside the
working day at both ends, so that an agent that only did what it was told would
get caught.
We worked out the expected result by hand.
One input can show a program wrong but can't show it right, so a pass here
means
only that none of them failed this particular test.

<<constants>>=
BOOKINGS = (
    "12:30-14:30\n09:00-10:30\n10:00-11:00\n13:00-13:30\n"
    "11:00-11:30\n07:00-08:30\n16:30-18:00\n"
)
EXPECTED = "08:30-09:00\n11:30-12:30\n14:30-16:30\n"
<<functions>>=
def is_correct(run):
    """Return whether the program in `run` gives the expected free slots."""
    done = subprocess.run(
        [sys.executable, str(run / "freeslots.py")],
        input=BOOKINGS,
        capture_output=True,
        text=True,
        timeout=10,
    )
    return done.stdout == EXPECTED
@

Counting lines is a crude measure, and we use it only for the amount of prose.
It can't tell a reason from a restatement of the code, which is why we read the
programs as well.
A documentation chunk starts with an [[@]] followed by a space or the end of
the
line, which is how noweb tells it from code such as a decorator, which also
starts with [[@]].
For the plain runs there is no [[.nw]] file, so all their lines count as code,
with the tests included as in the literate files.

<<functions>>=
def count_lines(run):
    """Return the numbers of non-blank prose and code lines in `run`."""
    source = run / "freeslots.nw"
    if not source.exists():
        code = (run / "freeslots.py").read_text().splitlines()
        tests = (run / "test_freeslots.py").read_text().splitlines()
        return 0, sum(1 for line in code + tests if line.strip())
    prose = code = 0
    in_code = False
    for line in source.read_text().splitlines():
        if re.match(r"@<<.*@>>=\s*$", line):
            in_code = True
        elif re.match(r"@(\s|$)", line):
            in_code = False
        elif line.strip():
            code += in_code
            prose += not in_code
    return prose, code
@

Finally, we look for the merging step by name.
That would miss the same logic under another name, so the counts were also
checked by reading the programs.

<<functions>>=
def has_merge_step(run):
    """Return whether the program in `run` has a function for merging."""
    return "def merge" in (run / "freeslots.py").read_text()
@

What the prose says is the more interesting difference.

\ltnote{%
  \textbf{Variation pattern}: contrast, on the same point in the same program:
  how malformed input is handled.
  We put them on one slide so that they can be compared directly, without
  saying
  in advance what to look for.
}%
Here both programs reject malformed input.
Compare how they tell the reader so.

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{exercise}[Same point, two programs]\label{ex:ai-why}
    With the noweb instruction:
    \inputminted[fontsize=\scriptsize,firstline=36,lastline=37]%
      {latex}{demo/noweb/1/freeslots.nw}
    With the skill as well:
    \inputminted[fontsize=\scriptsize,firstline=68,lastline=71]%
      {latex}{demo/skill/1/freeslots.nw}
    What does the second tell you that the first doesn't?
  \end{exercise}
\end{frame}

The first sentence restates the code: you could get it by reading the
[[raise]].
That is the kind of prose that, in \cref{sec:ai-agent-reader}, gave a model
nothing it didn't already have.
The second gives a reason you couldn't get from the code, namely what would go
wrong for the person using the tool.
That is the difference the skill asks for, and it held in the other runs as
well,
though not always as clearly.

\ltnote{%
  Relevant learning objectives: \cref{AiLOReader}.

  This is the deck's main corrective.
  So far the skill looks like an improvement, and students could conclude that
  more explanation means a better program.
  In all three skill runs the agent added a merging step that neither the plain
  nor the noweb runs had, and that the program doesn't need.
  We ask before telling, without using the word \enquote{unnecessary}.
}%
The third instruction changed something else, too.
Compare how the two programs find the free slots.

\begin{frame}[fragile,shrink=10]
  \begin{exercise}[Two designs]\label{ex:ai-design}
    With the plain instruction, the bookings are sorted directly:
    \inputminted[fontsize=\scriptsize,firstline=26,lastline=40]%
      {python}{demo/plain/1/freeslots.py}
    With the skill, the same function starts by calling this one instead:
    \inputminted[fontsize=\scriptsize,firstline=116,lastline=127]%
      {python}{demo/skill/1/freeslots.nw}
    Does the program need [[merge_slots]]?
  \end{exercise}
\end{frame}

It doesn't.
Sorting and keeping the furthest end seen so far already handle overlaps, as
the
plain version shows, and all nine programs give the same output.
All three skill runs added this step, and none of the others did.
My guess is that a pipeline of named stages makes a better story, and the skill
asks for a story.
The prose explains the step carefully, even noting that one of its choices is
\enquote{harmless for free-slot output}, but never asks whether the step itself
is needed.
A good explanation can make unnecessary code look justified, which is the risk
that \cref{sec:ai-checker} found, shown in small.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{remark}[What the demonstration shows]
    With one task and one model, the instruction changed what the prose
    explains
    and how the program is designed, but not what it does on our input.
    Prose that explains well is not evidence that the code should exist.
    You still have to read it.
  \end{remark}
\end{frame}

