\section{Related approaches}

\ltnote{%
  Relevant learning objectives: \cref{AiLOCompare}.

  \textbf{Variation pattern}: generalization, continued from the related
  approaches in the first lecture.
  \textbf{What stays invariant}: the two goals and the scale.
  \textbf{What varies}: the approach, now ones made for agents.
  Showboat is rated because it adds something none of the earlier approaches
  had, checking that the recorded outputs are real.
  Its limit is the one \cref{ex:ai-design} showed: there every output was
  correct, and the problem was in the design, which no output check catches.
}%
In the first lecture we rated notebooks, docstrings and pseudocode against the
goals of literate programming.
Agents have brought new approaches, and we rate two of them the same way.

The first is Showboat, by Simon Willison, a tool for an agent to show what it
has done.
In his words: \textcquote{willison2026showboat}{I need tools that allow agents
to clearly demonstrate their work to me, while minimizing the opportunities for
them to cheat about what they've done.}
An agent builds a Markdown document with commands: a note in prose, then a
command, which Showboat runs and records together with its output.
\textcquote{willison2026showboatreadme}{These documents serve as both readable
documentation and reproducible proof of work.
A verifier can re-execute all code blocks and confirm the outputs still match.}

\begin{frame}
  \begin{block}{Showboat}
    \begin{description}
      \item[\texttt{note}] The agent adds prose.
      \item[\texttt{exec}] Showboat runs a command and records it with its
        real output.
      \item[\texttt{verify}] Showboat runs every block again and reports any
        output that has changed.
    \end{description}
  \end{block}
  \begin{remark}
    The document is written for a person, and it can be checked by a
    program\autocite{willison2026showboatreadme}.
  \end{remark}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \only<presentation>{\LPgoals}
  \begin{exercise}\label{ex:ai-rate-showboat}
\begin{pycode}
ai_goals_poll(
    "How well do you think Showboat documents fulfil the goals of literate "
    "programming?",
    context=[
        AI_LP_GOALS,
        "Showboat: an agent writes a Markdown document of prose notes and "
        "commands. Showboat runs each command and records its real output, "
        "and verify runs them all again and reports any change.",
    ],
)
\end{pycode}
  \end{exercise}
\end{frame}

It does explain to people: the prose is written for a reader, and the outputs
are real.
But the order is the order in which the agent did things, not necessarily the
order best for understanding, which puts it closer to a notebook than to a
literate program.
What it adds is something literate programming lacks: the outputs are checked.
It doesn't check the prose, though, and Willison himself notes a way around it:
\textcquote{willison2026showboat}{Since the demo file is Markdown the agent
will
sometimes edit that file directly rather than using Showboat, which could
result in command outputs that don't reflect what actually
happened.}
We found no scientific evaluation of documents like these; the closest, a small
pilot with models as reviewers, found that requiring a delegation contract made
the work rate as more
reviewable to model reviewers, while every run was correct either
way\autocite{schmalbach2026contracts}.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{block}{Showboat}
    \begin{itemize}
      \item[\(+\)] {\color{green!75!black}\LPexplain}
      \item[\(\approx\)] {\color{orange}\LPorder}
    \end{itemize}
  \end{block}
  \begin{remark}
    In the order the agent worked.
    Outputs are checked, the prose is not.
  \end{remark}
\end{frame}

The second is the instruction file itself, together with skills and
specifications written before the code.
Specification-driven development, in tools such as GitHub's Spec Kit or
Amazon's
Kiro, writes the explanation first, as Knuth wanted, but in a separate file.
\Textcite{agarwal2026sdd}, who studied how it is practised, sum up its
evidence:
\textcquote[sec.~1]{agarwal2026sdd}{Tool
documentation, gray literature, and early research prescribe workflows and
promise better quality, less agent drift, and higher productivity.
However, their evaluations rarely extend beyond the authors' own projects},
and the practitioners they studied report that the specifications decay as the
code moves on.

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \only<presentation>{\LPgoals}
  \begin{exercise}\label{ex:ai-rate-instructions}
\begin{pycode}
ai_goals_poll(
    "How well do you think instruction files and specifications for agents "
    "fulfil the goals of literate programming?",
    context=[
        AI_LP_GOALS,
        "Instruction files (such as AGENTS.md or CLAUDE.md), skills and "
        "specifications: prose written for the agent, kept in files separate "
        "from the code.",
    ],
)
\end{pycode}
  \end{exercise}
\end{frame}

They explain, but mostly to the agent, and in a file of their own.
They are not in an order for understanding the program, since they aren't about
the program's parts at all, and they decay because nothing ties them to the
code.
A literate program keeps the explanation next to the code it explains, so the
two can change in the same edit; whether agents actually keep them in step is
one
of
the things nobody has measured.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{block}{Instruction files and specifications}
    \begin{itemize}
      \item[\(\approx\)] {\color{orange}\LPexplain}
      \item[\(-\)] {\color{red}\LPorder}
    \end{itemize}
  \end{block}
  \begin{remark}
    Written for the agent, separate from the code, and they go out of date.
  \end{remark}
\end{frame}


